// this is the end of the file.  The rest are notes.

JTT spectral decomposition, transcribed verbatim from the arrays
`jtteigs` and `jttprobs` embedded in PHYLIP 3.697 `protdist.c`
("this jtt matrix decomposition due to Elisabeth Tillier").

Layout: 20 eigenvalues, then the 20x20 spectral coefficient matrix C
(row-major; columns in ARNDCQEGHILKMFPSTWYV order). C[0] is the
equilibrium frequency vector pi (sums to ~1.0000005); c[m][a] equals
sqrt(pi_a) V[a,m] where V is the eigenvector matrix of the symmetrized
rate matrix Pi^(1/2) Q Pi^(-1/2), so that the joint site probability is
pi_a P_ab(t) = sum_m C[m,a] C[m,b] exp(lam_m t) — the formula protdist
evaluates in predict().

Why this source (v1.1 Task 6): PAML's dat/jones.dat is a different JTT
variant (Kosiol & Goldman 2005, "Different versions of the Dayhoff rate
matrix"): its frequencies and exchangeabilities differ enough to shift
ML distances ~0.5% (~2e-3), failing the protdist parity fixtures by far.
The reference corpus and doc matrix were generated by protdist itself,
whose JTT is this embedded decomposition. Constants derived from the
reconstructed rate matrix lose parity (residual ~2.3e-6 > 5e-7 doc
tolerance) because the embedded data carries ~1e-7 precision noise;
shipping the spectral coefficients verbatim is the only representation
that reproduces the oracle to its printed precision.
